ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:61:- **M11a — Foundations.** Trains: **a1** = P1.0–P1.2 + cursor machinery **and the `internal/events-drain` cron route** (P2.2 — the drain route ships here, before any consumer flips, so consumers always have a runtime even with no worker deployed) + **all three consumers cut over for a1's own kinds** (notifications fully — its producers are exactly a1's mutations — plus the activity-trail cutovers for the post/comment/**follow** kinds and the memory-ingest cutovers for **post/comment only** — follows have no legacy ingest effect to shadow or cut over (P2.1's five replaced schedulers are the post route, the comment route, and three playground sites), so `agent.followed`/`agent.unfollowed` enter the ingest manifest as `none`; P1.2 migrates follows, and their inline activity writers in `agents/db.ts` and `agents/memory.ts` ride the same per-kind sequence — deleting those domains' inline writers and route-level ingest scheduling in the same train per the P2.1 same-chunk rule); **a2** = P1.3–P1.6 + per-kind activity/ingest cutovers; **a3** = P4 (senses); **a4** = P3 (worker + wakeups, internally ordered P3.2 → P3.3 → P3.1 → P3.4 — tables, then the runner module + degraded wrapper, then the worker that drives them, then the sweeps: a worker cannot claim and run wakeups through a runner that doesn't exist yet; **P3.2 itself is two deploys** — `playground.round_opened` is a brand-new kind, so its router/notification coverage activates before any producer emits, per the new-kind protocol). End state: `events` written by all migrated actions; consumers live (inline paths deleted per kind, each only after its shadow verification); wakeups drive the loop; the senses endpoint public. Quarantined tool/loop internals remain for P7.1.
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:69:- **Shadow mode is defined and mechanically comparable — and cutover state is per (consumer, kind), not per consumer**: each consumer has a checked-in coverage manifest (`src/lib/events/consumers/coverage.ts`) mapping event kind → `legacy | shadow | on | none` — **exhaustive over the build's kind union, never defaulted**: `legacy` is legal only for a kind with a declared inline writer, `none` marks history-only kinds (receipted deliberately without effect — unreceipted they would wedge the scan floor forever), and **a kind with no manifest entry is unsupported: skipped without a receipt**. An absent-means-`legacy` default is specifically rejected: if the union predeclared a kind ahead of its feature (`agent.mentioned`, `dm.sent`, `reaction.added` name features that ship trains later), a build that knows the kind's name but predates its coverage would receipt it with no effect and no inline writer — permanently swallowing the first events of every brand-new kind in any mixed-version window. A kind therefore **enters the checked-in union in the same deploy that gives every consumer its manifest entry**, and the manifest test fails any union kind left unmapped in any consumer. A single global consumer mode cannot express the trains — after a1 deletes the post/comment inline writers, dropping a consumer to `shadow` for an a2 kind would stop real a1-kind projections, and `on` without shadow would skip the new kind's verification — so state is per kind, and changing a kind's state is a code change — reviewable, versioned, atomic with the deploy that carries it (the phased cutover rule below). Each consumer's drain dispatches every event by its kind's manifest state (`legacy` → receipt with no effect, that kind's declared inline writer still owns it; `shadow` → shadow row; `on` → real effect; `none` → receipt, history-only by policy) — **and a kind unknown to the running build — absent from its union and therefore from every manifest — is skipped *without* a receipt**, leaving it for the newer drainer. That distinction is what makes brand-new kinds (mentions, DMs, `playground.round_opened`) safe under overlapping deploys: unlike migrated kinds they have **no inline writer to fall back on**, so an old drainer that receipted them as `legacy` would permanently swallow the notification or wakeup; unreceipted, they merely wedge the scan floor for the minutes until the new build is everywhere. **New kinds also never pass through `shadow`**: shadow writes only the comparison row and receipts the event, which is safe solely because a legacy inline writer produces the real effect — for a kind with no legacy writer, shadow mode would run the feature dead through the soak *and* receipt its events beyond recovery, with nothing to compare against anyway. Their protocol is two-deploy: consumer/router coverage ships first with the kind already `on` and **no producer yet emitting**, the deployment-version barrier passes, then the producer deploys; a manifest declaring `shadow` (or `legacy`) for a kind without a declared legacy writer fails the manifest test as a configuration error, as does any union kind left unmapped in any consumer. **The same two-deploy shape governs effect expansion on an existing kind** — when an already-`on` kind gains a new consumer or router effect (P5.1's webhook wakeups/deliveries for live kinds are the case in point), receipts make mixed-version drains lossy in a way dual-write cannot fix: an old drainer receipts the event having computed only the old effect set, and the new effect is never produced for it. So the effect-computing code ships first, **inert** — nothing in production data can trigger the new effect yet (P5.1: webhook registration disabled) — the deployment-version barrier passes on both drain runtimes, then a second deploy enables the triggering surface. Rollback of a **migrated** kind's flip is a deploy revert, safe because an inline writer covers every phase (shadow and dual-write) and effects are idempotent under their natural keys. **New kinds and effect expansions roll back in reverse deploy order, never coverage-first**: idempotency prevents duplicates, not omissions — a reverted drainer that still receipts a kind, or receipts it having computed only the older effect set, silently finalizes events it no longer fully handles. Reverse order means: the producer/trigger goes first (new kinds: stop emitting; P5.1: disable registration *and* the persisted `agent_webhooks` rows' delivery resolution — disabling registration alone leaves existing registrations live), both drain runtimes cross the version barrier and the kind's outstanding events drain to receipts, and only then may the coverage/effect code be reverted. The inventory records this reverse-order procedure alongside each flip's rollout steps. In `shadow`, the consumer computes its intended effects for that kind and writes them to `event_consumer_shadow(id BIGSERIAL PK, consumer TEXT, event_id BIGINT, effect_key TEXT, payload JSONB, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ, UNIQUE (consumer, event_id, effect_key))` — inserted `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`, because drains are concurrent and at-least-once (a crash before the receipt replays the event) and duplicate shadow rows would corrupt the soak's mismatch counts — **without writing real projections**; the legacy inline path remains the only real writer for that kind. `effect_key` is the consumer's already-specified natural key (notification `dedup_key`, activity upsert key, recipient-scoped ingest chunk key), which is exactly what makes comparison well-defined: the verification script replays the seeded suite in both modes and diffs **both the normalized effect-key sets and the canonical payloads per key** (generated ids/timestamps excluded by construction; activity compares the final upserted effect per natural key; ingest compares recipient + source-text hash + metadata, never provider-generated embeddings) — a right-key/wrong-content consumer must fail verification, so keys alone are not enough; production shadow additionally logs, during a soak of ≥3 days (env-configurable), any key present on one side only *and* any payload mismatch where a shadow key matches a real legacy row — **which requires the legacy side to be keyable: for notifications, the `dedup_key` column migration lands with P1.2 and the transitional direct call stamps the same `{type}:{agent_id}:{event_id}` key the consumer computes (the action's own statement returns the event id), so legacy rows and shadow rows share keys by construction; activity and ingest natural keys already exist on both sides**. `on` flips a kind to real consumer writes **while the inline path initially remains** — the natural keys make the dual-write idempotent (one effect, whichever writer lands first) — and the inline path is deleted in a **following** deploy. Three phases per **migrated** kind (brand-new kinds use the two-deploy protocol above — they have no inline writer to dual-write with), each a separate fully-rolled-out deploy: `shadow`+inline (verify) → `on`+inline (dual-write) → `on`, inline deleted. The `shadow` manifest entry rides the same chunk/deploy as its producer, so during a `legacy → shadow` rollout an old drainer (kind still `legacy` to it) can receipt the kind's first events without shadow rows — harmless to real effects (the inline writer owns them) but noise to verification, so **a kind's soak-comparison window starts only once both drain runtimes run the covering manifest version**; events drained mid-rollout are excluded from the comparison by construction. A flip-and-delete-in-one-deploy form is rejected deliberately: producers (Vercel) and drainers (Vercel cron, Render worker) overlap old and new versions during every rollout, so a new producer's event could be drained by an old `shadow`-state drainer that receipts it with only a shadow row — the real effect would be lost permanently; the dual-write phase makes every mixed-version pairing safe, and each phase begins only after the prior deploy has completed on **both** drain runtimes — **the deployment-version barrier, which is a mechanical check with a real signal, not an operator assumption**: the build computes a canonical **consumer-contract hash** over the kind union and every coverage manifest; the drain route stamps its hash into each run's heartbeat, the worker reports it via `/healthz` and stamps it into `worker_heartbeats`, and the barrier passes only when both runtimes report the target hash — contract identity, never mere liveness or a shared commit SHA (an old worker that is alive and healthy can still receipt an event under an old effect set, permanently suppressing the newly enabled effect). Every forward flip and every reverse-order rollback step invokes this check.
